  ]}
]} | tojson }}</script>
</head><body class="store">
{{ header("smarthome") }}
<main id="main" class="frame">
  <section class="memory-hero guide-hero" aria-labelledby="sh-title">
    <div class="memory-copy">
      <a class="crumb light-crumb" href="/">← Back to DealFactory</a>
      <div class="label light"><span class="rule"></span>COMPATIBILITY GUIDE</div>
      <h1 id="sh-title">Smart home &amp; <em>appliances.</em></h1>
      <p>The four specs that cause most returns in European homes — and how to check them against what you already own before you buy.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="cta" href="/advisor/?preset=apple-opentherm&amp;need=Matter%20thermostat%20with%20OpenTherm#connect">Check against your setup <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="ghost" href="#protocols">Jump to checklist <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><button class="dark-button" id="share-guide" type="button" hidden>Share this guide ↗</button></div>
    </div>
    <figure class="memory-card" aria-label="Quick compatibility spec sheet">
      <div class="memory-card-head"><strong>4 things to log once</strong><span>Spec sheet</span></div>
      <ul class="owned-list">
        <li><i aria-hidden="true">01</i><div><b>Ecosystem &amp; hubs</b><small>Apple Home, Google Home, Home Assistant, Hue</small></div><span class="source">Protocols</span></li>
        <li><i aria-hidden="true">02</i><div><b>Boiler &amp; heating</b><small>OpenTherm, on/off, or district heating</small></div><span class="source">Heating</span></li>
        <li><i aria-hidden="true">03</i><div><b>TV ports &amp; mount</b><small>HDMI eARC, VESA pattern, max weight</small></div><span class="source">AV</span></li>
        <li><i aria-hidden="true">04</i><div><b>Exact model codes</b><small>Air purifier, vacuum &amp; espresso machine</small></div><span class="source">Parts</span></li>
      </ul>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="protocols">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>01 · SMART HOME ECOSYSTEMS</div>
    <h2>Matter, Thread and Zigbee: check the hub first</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>Matter over Thread vs. Wi-Fi</h3><p>Matter over Thread is fast and low-power, but requires a Thread Border Router (Apple TV 4K, HomePod mini, Nest Hub 2nd gen, or a Home Assistant dongle). Matter over Wi-Fi works with your router alone.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Zigbee &amp; Hue Bridges</h3><p>Philips Hue, IKEA Dirigera and Aqara hubs each expose different subsets of third-party Zigbee devices. Confirm whether a sensor or bulb pairs directly with your bridge before ordering.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Local control &amp; subscriptions</h3><p>Doorbells, cameras and thermostats often hide basic history or automations behind monthly plans. Check whether local control (HomeKit, Matter or Home Assistant) works without a subscription.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="heating">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>02 · HEATING &amp; APPLIANCES</div>
    <h2>Thermostats, TVs and replacement parts</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>OpenTherm vs. on/off boilers</h3><p>Most modern Dutch and EU boilers (Intergas, Remeha, Nefit, Vaillant) modulate water temperature over OpenTherm. A basic on/off thermostat loses that efficiency — check your boiler manual for OpenTherm or brand-specific bridges.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Soundbars &amp; TV mounts</h3><p>For Dolby Atmos without lip-sync delay, confirm your TV has an <strong>HDMI eARC</strong> port (not just optical or ARC). For wall mounts, match both the VESA bolt spacing (e.g. 300×200) and the TV weight.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Filters &amp; consumables</h3><p>Air purifiers, robot vacuums and espresso machines change filter shapes between revisions. Record the exact model code from the sticker once so every future refill order is verified against it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="tado-x">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>03 · TADO X, V3+ AND OPENTHERM</div>
    <h2>tado X does not extend a V3+ system</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>Two generations</h3><p>tado X thermostats, radiator knobs and the Bridge X are a separate generation from tado V3+. Retailers list them as not compatible with older tado products. Replacing one V3+ knob with an X knob does not join the old rooms.</p></div>
      <div><h3>OpenTherm</h3><p>On a modulating boiler (Intergas, Remeha, Nefit, Vaillant and others), OpenTherm lets the thermostat request a water temperature instead of only on/off. Confirm OpenTherm, or the boiler’s own bridge, on the model plate before you order. A Remeha eTwist without the gateway is for Remeha Ace boilers; other Remeha series need the gateway version.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Matter and the paid extras</h3><p>tado X is listed with Matter alongside Apple Home, Google Home and Alexa. Some automations, including geofencing, are sold as tado Auto-Assist. Check the current subscription on tado’s site; the thermostat still sets a schedule without it.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="link-row"><a href="/advisor/?preset=apple-opentherm&amp;need=OpenTherm%20thermostat%20for%20Apple%20Home%20that%20is%20not%20tado%20V3%2B#connect">Check an OpenTherm thermostat against Apple Home <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="p1-meter">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>04 · SLIMME METER AND DYNAMIC TARIFFS</div>
    <h2>The P1 port is usage, not the hourly price</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>DSMR P1</h3><p>Dutch smart meters expose a P1 port using the DSMR telegram. A reader such as the HomeWizard P1 Meter sits on that port and publishes electricity and gas use on your home network. It does not, by itself, know what your supplier charges this hour.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Dynamic contracts</h3><p>Hourly or quarter-hour prices come from the energy contract (the supplier’s API or app), not from the meter. A plug or thermostat can shift load only if that price feed is connected — for example through HomeWizard Energy, Home Assistant, or the supplier’s own integration.</p></div>
      <div><h3>What to write down</h3><p>Note the meter type, whether you already have a P1 reader, and the contract (fixed, variable, or dynamic). A “works with dynamic prices” claim needs both the reader and the tariff source.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="link-row"><a href="/advisor/?need=P1%20meter%20reader%20for%20a%20Dutch%20slimme%20meter%20and%20Home%20Assistant#connect">Find a P1 reader for this meter <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="matter-appliances">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>05 · APPLIANCES AND MATTER</div>
    <h2>A Matter badge is not the full appliance</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>Limited clusters</h3><p>Matter on a washer, dryer, robot vacuum or air purifier often exposes power and a few states. Cycle lists, maps, filter hours and voice routines may stay in the manufacturer’s app. Read the device’s Matter feature list, not only the logo.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Filters still use a model code</h3><p>Philips NanoProtect filters are matched by code, not by “Matter”. FY0900/30 is listed for the AC0920; FY1700/30 is listed for the AC1715. FY1410/30 and FY2422/30 are different parts. Copy the code from the purifier label.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Local versus cloud</h3><p>A Matter connection can keep basic control on your home network. Firmware, history and some energy features may still require the vendor account. Treat “no cloud” as a claim to verify on the product page.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="link-row"><a href="/advisor/?need=HEPA%20filter%20for%20my%20Philips%20air%20purifier#connect">Match a filter to your purifier <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="field-notes" id="thread-routers">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>06 · THREAD BORDER ROUTERS</div>
    <h2>Matter over Thread needs one border router</h2>
    <div class="notes-grid">
      <div><h3>Apple</h3><p>HomePod mini, HomePod (2nd generation) and Apple TV 4K from 2021 onward can be a Thread border router for Apple Home. An older Apple TV without Thread is not a substitute. Confirm the generation on the device before you buy Thread sensors.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Google, Aqara, Home Assistant</h3><p>Google Nest Hub (2nd gen) and Nest Wifi Pro are Thread border routers. The Aqara Hub M3 advertises Matter, Zigbee and Thread. Home Assistant Connect ZBT-1 (and Home Assistant Yellow) can run a Thread border router. One of these is enough for Matter-over-Thread devices such as the Aqara Door and Window Sensor P2.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Wi-Fi Matter is different</h3><p>A Matter-over-Wi-Fi plug, such as the Shelly Plug S MTR Gen3, joins the Wi-Fi network and does not need a Thread border router. Do not buy a Thread router only because a Wi-Fi Matter device says Matter.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="link-row"><a href="/advisor/?preset=ha-zigbee&amp;need=Thread%20border%20router%20for%20Home%20Assistant#connect">Check a border router for Home Assistant <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
  </section>

  <aside class="advisor-strip">
    <div><div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>PERSONAL CHECK</div><h2>Save your home setup <em>once.</em></h2><p>Add your hubs, boiler and appliances (or import past receipts from Gmail) and ask DealFactory what fits your exact models.</p></div>
    <a class="dark-button" href="/advisor/?preset=ha-zigbee#connect">Build your setup <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </aside>

  <section class="faq" aria-labelledby="sh-faq-title">
    <div class="label"><span class="rule"></span>QUESTIONS</div>
    <h2 id="sh-faq-title">Common fit questions<span>.</span></h2>
    <div class="faq-list">{% for question, answer in faqs %}<details><summary>{{ question }}</summary><p>{{ answer }}</p></details>{% endfor %}</div>
  </section>
</main>{{ footer() }}</body></html>
